1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,308
One tool is easy to write by hand.

2
00:00:03,658 --> 00:00:09,289
Six tools, each with a schema, a
description, a permission and a timeout, is where

3
00:00:09,289 --> 00:00:12,104
hand-written dictionaries start to break.

4
00:00:12,454 --> 00:00:18,141
In this lesson, we'll organise many
tools in one place, let Pydantic write the

5
00:00:18,141 --> 00:00:23,318
schemas for us, and handle a model
that asks for several tools at once.

6
00:00:25,118 --> 00:00:27,667
You'll register tools with a decorator.

7
00:00:28,267 --> 00:00:32,199
Generate strict JSON
schemas from Pydantic classes.

8
00:00:32,799 --> 00:00:34,942
Dispatch a tool call by its name.

9
00:00:35,542 --> 00:00:38,302
And handle several tool calls in one turn.

10
00:00:40,102 --> 00:00:41,863
First, what is a registry?

11
00:00:42,213 --> 00:00:43,983
Think of a restaurant menu.

12
00:00:44,333 --> 00:00:48,592
Every dish is listed once, with
its name, description and price.

13
00:00:48,942 --> 00:00:52,458
When an order comes in, the
kitchen looks it up on the menu.

14
00:00:52,808 --> 00:00:56,012
A tool registry is
the menu for your agent.

15
00:00:56,362 --> 00:01:01,683
Every tool is listed once, with its
name, description, input form, permission, and

16
00:01:01,683 --> 00:01:02,202
timeout.

17
00:01:02,552 --> 00:01:06,169
When the model asks for a
tool, your code looks it up there.

18
00:01:06,769 --> 00:01:07,702
Why bother?

19
00:01:08,052 --> 00:01:10,591
Because one list
means one source of truth.

20
00:01:10,941 --> 00:01:16,375
The definitions you send to the model,
and the functions your code runs, can never

21
00:01:16,375 --> 00:01:17,170
drift apart.

22
00:01:18,970 --> 00:01:20,794
Here's how the project does it.

23
00:01:21,144 --> 00:01:26,774
A Tool is a frozen dataclass that
holds everything about one tool: its name,

24
00:01:26,774 --> 00:01:32,701
description, the Pydantic class for its
input, the function, its permission, its

25
00:01:32,701 --> 00:01:35,887
timeout, and an
optional confirmation text.

26
00:01:36,237 --> 00:01:38,499
We'll use permission in lesson five.

27
00:01:39,099 --> 00:01:44,217
The registry is just a dictionary,
from the tool's name to its Tool object.

28
00:01:44,817 --> 00:01:46,705
And tool is a decorator.

29
00:01:47,055 --> 00:01:50,433
A decorator is a function
that wraps another function.

30
00:01:50,783 --> 00:01:53,368
This one doesn't
change the function at all.

31
00:01:53,718 --> 00:01:58,859
It just adds it to the registry, using
the function's own name as the tool name.

32
00:02:00,659 --> 00:02:02,064
Now one real tool.

33
00:02:02,414 --> 00:02:05,082
First, the input as a Pydantic class.

34
00:02:05,432 --> 00:02:09,187
A title between one and a
hundred and twenty characters.

35
00:02:09,537 --> 00:02:10,947
A due date, or None.

36
00:02:11,297 --> 00:02:14,486
And a priority that
must be low, normal, or high.

37
00:02:15,086 --> 00:02:20,233
Then the decorator, with the
description, the input class, and the permission:

38
00:02:20,233 --> 00:02:23,004
write, because adding a task changes data.

39
00:02:23,604 --> 00:02:25,072
And the function itself.

40
00:02:25,422 --> 00:02:31,019
Notice that it receives an AddTask
object, already checked, not a raw dictionary.

41
00:02:31,369 --> 00:02:37,539
So inside the function, a dot due really
is a date, and a dot priority really is one

42
00:02:37,539 --> 00:02:39,008
of the three values.

43
00:02:40,808 --> 00:02:45,277
Writing JSON schemas by hand
is slow, and easy to get wrong.

44
00:02:45,627 --> 00:02:47,594
So we let Pydantic write them.

45
00:02:47,944 --> 00:02:53,468
Here's a note with a string title, a
string text, a boolean pinned, and a colour

46
00:02:53,468 --> 00:02:55,954
that must be yellow, blue, or green.

47
00:02:56,554 --> 00:02:59,695
Schema for turns the
class into a JSON schema.

48
00:03:00,045 --> 00:03:05,268
Look at the right side: string, string,
boolean, and an enum with the three colours.

49
00:03:05,618 --> 00:03:11,040
Every field is required, and
additional properties is false, which strict mode

50
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:11,457
needs.

51
00:03:13,257 --> 00:03:14,784
How does it work inside?

52
00:03:15,134 --> 00:03:20,573
Pydantic's schema contains a few
keywords that strict tool schemas don't accept,

53
00:03:20,573 --> 00:03:21,933
like maximum length.

54
00:03:22,283 --> 00:03:23,589
So we remove them.

55
00:03:23,939 --> 00:03:28,831
That's safe, because Pydantic still
checks those limits when the input arrives.

56
00:03:29,431 --> 00:03:33,115
This code walks through the
schema and drops those keywords.

57
00:03:33,465 --> 00:03:39,655
One detail: Pydantic adds a keyword
called title, but our task also has a field

58
00:03:39,655 --> 00:03:40,674
called title.

59
00:03:41,024 --> 00:03:43,357
So property names are kept, always.

60
00:03:43,707 --> 00:03:49,391
I actually got this wrong in my first
version, and a test caught it: the title field

61
00:03:49,391 --> 00:03:50,001
vanished.

62
00:03:50,601 --> 00:03:56,762
Then, for every object, additional
properties becomes false, and every field becomes

63
00:03:56,762 --> 00:03:57,422
required.

64
00:03:57,772 --> 00:04:02,514
Optional values, like the due date,
are written as either a date or null.

65
00:04:03,114 --> 00:04:07,300
And definitions builds the
list for the API, from the registry.

66
00:04:07,650 --> 00:04:11,422
Name, description, schema,
and strict true, for every tool.

67
00:04:13,222 --> 00:04:14,614
Here's the whole menu.

68
00:04:14,964 --> 00:04:20,145
Six tools: add, list, complete and
delete tasks, and add and search notes.

69
00:04:20,495 --> 00:04:23,609
Each with its inputs, its
permission, and its timeout.

70
00:04:23,959 --> 00:04:26,539
One registry, so nothing can drift.

71
00:04:28,339 --> 00:04:30,220
Next, parallel tool calls.

72
00:04:30,570 --> 00:04:33,141
Imagine your table orders three dishes.

73
00:04:33,491 --> 00:04:38,487
A good waiter takes all three
orders in one trip, not one trip per dish.

74
00:04:38,837 --> 00:04:40,489
A model can do the same.

75
00:04:40,839 --> 00:04:46,688
When a request needs several independent
things, one reply can contain several tool

76
00:04:46,688 --> 00:04:47,464
use blocks.

77
00:04:47,814 --> 00:04:52,014
Your code runs them all, and
sends all the results back together.

78
00:04:53,814 --> 00:04:55,859
The request asks for two tasks.

79
00:04:56,209 --> 00:04:58,834
The reply contains two tool use blocks.

80
00:04:59,184 --> 00:05:00,890
That's a parallel tool call.

81
00:05:01,490 --> 00:05:07,598
We run each call in order, and each
result keeps the ID of the call it answers:

82
00:05:07,598 --> 00:05:10,613
offline zero one, and offline zero two.

83
00:05:11,213 --> 00:05:17,176
And here's the rule developers often
break: all the results go into one single user

84
00:05:17,176 --> 00:05:19,762
message, not one message per result.

85
00:05:20,112 --> 00:05:25,373
The next request sends three
messages, and the last one holds both results.

86
00:05:27,173 --> 00:05:29,174
Let's dry run the message list.

87
00:05:29,524 --> 00:05:32,183
Message one, from the user: the request.

88
00:05:32,783 --> 00:05:38,900
Message two, from the assistant: two
tool use blocks, IDs zero one and zero two.

89
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:45,354
Message three, from the user: both tool
results, zero one and zero two, together.

90
00:05:45,954 --> 00:05:50,005
And the wrong way: two
separate user messages, one per result.

91
00:05:50,355 --> 00:05:51,402
Don't do that.

92
00:05:51,752 --> 00:05:55,410
Keep the results together,
in the same order as the calls.

93
00:05:57,210 --> 00:05:58,702
Pause and work it out.

94
00:05:59,052 --> 00:06:01,172
A request needs three tool calls.

95
00:06:01,522 --> 00:06:06,167
The model asks for all three in
its first reply, and then answers.

96
00:06:06,767 --> 00:06:08,399
How many requests are sent?

97
00:06:08,749 --> 00:06:11,735
And how many messages
are in the list at the end?

98
00:06:18,335 --> 00:06:19,383
Two requests.

99
00:06:19,733 --> 00:06:25,314
And four messages: the user request, the
assistant with three tool calls, the user

100
00:06:25,314 --> 00:06:28,105
with three results, and the final answer.

101
00:06:28,455 --> 00:06:34,068
Compare that with lesson three of
Module 4, where one call per turn needed more

102
00:06:34,068 --> 00:06:34,921
round trips.

103
00:06:36,721 --> 00:06:37,862
Interview corner.

104
00:06:38,212 --> 00:06:41,592
Why generate tool
schemas from Pydantic models?

105
00:06:44,192 --> 00:06:46,562
A good answer: one source of truth.

106
00:06:46,912 --> 00:06:52,648
The same class describes the input to
the model, and validates it in your code, so

107
00:06:52,648 --> 00:06:54,467
the two can't drift apart.

108
00:06:54,817 --> 00:06:57,883
And less hand-written
JSON means fewer mistakes.

109
00:06:58,233 --> 00:07:01,156
The trap: thinking the
schema alone protects you.

110
00:07:01,506 --> 00:07:04,422
You still validate
every input when it arrives.

111
00:07:05,022 --> 00:07:08,593
Follow-up: the model
returns two tool use blocks.

112
00:07:08,943 --> 00:07:10,291
How do you respond?

113
00:07:12,891 --> 00:07:19,062
Run both, then send one user message,
containing two tool results, each with its

114
00:07:19,062 --> 00:07:19,988
matching ID.

115
00:07:20,338 --> 00:07:25,106
The trap: one message per
result, or answering only the first call.

116
00:07:25,456 --> 00:07:27,186
The next request will fail.

117
00:07:28,986 --> 00:07:29,845
Quick check.

118
00:07:30,195 --> 00:07:32,874
A reply contains three tool use blocks.

119
00:07:33,224 --> 00:07:35,484
What does the next user message contain?

120
00:07:41,684 --> 00:07:47,345
Three tool result blocks, each with
its matching ID, all in that one message.

121
00:07:49,145 --> 00:07:49,952
Recap.

122
00:07:50,302 --> 00:07:54,683
A registry lists every tool
once, so there's one source of truth.

123
00:07:55,283 --> 00:07:59,127
The tool decorator
registers a function by its name.

124
00:07:59,727 --> 00:08:02,968
Pydantic classes
generate the strict schemas.

125
00:08:03,568 --> 00:08:09,248
And for parallel calls, run them all,
and return all the results in one message.

126
00:08:09,848 --> 00:08:13,373
Next lesson: the model's
input can still be wrong.

127
00:08:13,723 --> 00:08:19,110
You'll validate every call, and turn
mistakes into messages the model can fix.
